---
// The whole ceremony — generate, hand off, delete, register — is one command.
---

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="breakglass" data-reveal>
      <div class="breakglass__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--amber">Break-glass keys</p>
          <h3>Set up disaster recovery on day one.</h3>
          <p>
            A break-glass key is a separate emergency age key pair — <b
              >portable</b
            > by design, stored <b>offline</b> in a vault and shared with only a few
            admins. If every developer's Secure Enclave or YubiKey is lost, it can
            still decrypt and re-key the repository. An interactive <code
              class="mono amber">sopsy init</code
            > sets it up by default. <code class="mono amber"
              >sopsy recipient break-glass</code
            > runs the whole ceremony in one command: generate, hand off, wait for
            ENTER, delete the local copies, register and re-key.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top:1rem">
            Lose your only hardware key with no break-glass key, and every
            secret becomes <b>permanently undecryptable</b>. That's why <code
              class="mono amber">doctor</code
            > and <code class="mono amber">check</code> nag until one exists — and
            sopsy refuses to remove the sole break-glass recipient. The same guided
            ceremony powers <code class="mono amber">sopsy recipient ci</code> for
            your pipeline — but the two keys must <b>never</b> be the same: offline/few-hands
            and online/every-run are opposite threat models.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="terminal">
            <div class="terminal__bar">
              <div class="terminal__dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
              <span class="terminal__title">offline · once</span>
            </div>
            <div class="terminal__body">
              <span class="ln cmt"
                ># one guided ceremony: generate → store → delete → register</span
              >
              <span class="ln prompt"
                >sopsy recipient break-glass -o break-glass</span
              >
              <span class="ln ok">
                ✔ wrote break-glass.private + break-glass.public</span
              >
              <span class="ln warn">
                ⚠ copy both into 1Password / a vault, then continue</span
              >
              <span class="ln cmt"> press ENTER once the keys are saved …</span>
              <span class="ln ok"> ✔ deleted both local key files</span>
              <span class="ln ok"> ✔ registered break-glass recipient</span>
              <span class="ln ok">
                ✔ re-keyed every secret (sops updatekeys)</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
